# Copy to `.env` and fill in. `.env` is gitignored; this template is committed.
#
# Why `.env` and not `.env.local`: Next.js reads both, but the Prisma CLI reads
# only what dotenv loads in prisma.config.ts, which is `.env`. One file keeps the
# app and the migration CLI from disagreeing about the database.

# --- Database -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Host port for the container. Defaults to 5432, but a native/WSL Postgres often
# already holds that port and will silently shadow Docker's publish — you then
# get "password authentication failed" while the container itself is healthy.
# 5433 is the safe default here. Read by docker-compose.yml too, so change both
# this and the URLs below together.
POSTGRES_PORT=5433

# Local development points at the Docker container (`pnpm db:up`); credentials
# match docker-compose.yml. Nothing secret here — it is a throwaway local server.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://applyflow:applyflow@localhost:5433/applyflow?schema=public"

# Integration tests get their own database on the same server so they can
# truncate freely without wiping the database you develop against.
TEST_DATABASE_URL="postgresql://applyflow:applyflow@localhost:5433/applyflow_test?schema=public"

# In production this is Neon. Use Neon's UNPOOLED connection string when running
# `prisma migrate deploy` — schema changes cannot go through pgbouncer.

# --- Clerk --------------------------------------------------------------------
# From the Clerk dashboard: https://dashboard.clerk.com → API keys.
# The secret key is a real credential: never commit it, never log it.
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY="pk_test_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx"
CLERK_SECRET_KEY="sk_test_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx"
